add(TYW,'Trade Your Way to Financial Freedom','Book',['psychology','money-management','rule-based'], 'A system-development book connecting beliefs and objectives with expectancy, entries, exits, opportunity and position sizing. The reviewed passages provide a concrete 55/21 channel method and a random-entry volatility-trail experiment, while treating position sizing as a separate driver of portfolio behaviour.', 'partial_rules','Van K. Tharp',1999,[(tyr,[[6,8]],'Title, copyright 1999, ISBN 0-07-064762-3 and contents checked.'),(tyr,[[116,116]],'Printed pages 200–201: source wording is a 10-day exponential moving average of the average true range; three-volatility initial and close-based one-way trailing stops.'),(tyr,[[144,145]],'Printed pages 256–259: daily close-based favourable-only volatility trail; page 258 separately discusses Wilder’s suggested range and weekly-volatility alternatives, without replacing page 200’s EMA10 wording.'),(tyr,[[159,159]],'Printed pages 286–287: 55-day stop-order highs/lows and daily 21-day adverse-extreme trailing exit; ten commodities, 1981–1991.'),(tyr,[[165,165]],'Printed pages 296–297 distinguish a 10-day simple volatility example from a 20-day average-range sizing table; neither silently replaces the random-entry EMA10.')], [('Objectives and beliefs','Define what the system must accomplish before optimizing signals.'),('Expectancy and opportunity','Separate edge per unit risk from the frequency and cost of opportunities.'),('Entry and exit components','55/21 channels and random entries are separately described source examples.'),('Position sizing','Compare amount-at-risk and volatility sizing without equating either to an entry rule.')], [random10,breakout])
